<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahajanga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Majunga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahajanga, formerly Majunga, is a port city on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> at the mouth of the Betsiboka River. It grew as a town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalaotra">Antalaotra</a> merchants and in the 18th century became the capital and main port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakalava">Sakalava</a> kingdom of Boina. The Merina conquered it in 1824, and French forces landed there in 1895 for the conquest of Madagascar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahajanga.jpg" alt="Mahajanga" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis de Chambure · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahajanga_cathedrale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Since the 10th century the ports of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, home to an Islamized population, had drawn merchants and migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and the Far East; by the late 15th century the Arab geographer Ibn Mājid identified at least a dozen of them. In the early eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriamandisoarivo">Andriamandisoarivo</a>, backed by an army of eight hundred men, founded a new Sakalava dynasty in the north called Volamena. He died at Bezavo around 1710. His kingdom held the established trading port of Mazalagem Nova and the new ports of Boina and Mahajanga.</p><p>An English captain described Mahajanga in 1764 as a bustling, cosmopolitan port, built after the Indian fashion, with many stone buildings and mosques, and with Muslim inhabitants from Surate, Johana, Mosembeck and the Commoro islands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakalava">Sakalava</a> rulers let Muslims practise their religion freely and in return imposed trading controls through a purser who traded in the king's name. Sakalava kings and queens also allowed European merchants to trade, and some incorporated Islamic beliefs into their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship">divine kingship</a> and had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> by the early nineteenth century.</p><p>In the 1780s, according to the French traveller Dumaine, about 200 Muslim traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> had settled at Mahajanga at the mouth of the Betsiboka River, mostly Khojas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismailis</a> and Da'udi Bohras, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> coming later. Their legal status was confused: they often declared themselves Malagasy subjects to avoid laws against slave-holding and stone houses that bound British subjects, and their dhows flew French flags. The 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> found 4,480 Indians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. The word Mahajanga may have an Indic etymology. In 1977 the city saw ethnic unrest that forced the evacuation of the Comorian minority living there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mahajanga is a seaport on the north-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and the capital of the Boeny Region; the district, which is identical with the city, had 258,068 inhabitants in 2020. Only small ships can reach the wharf, and deeper ships anchor off and transfer cargo by barge. The city is the seat of a Roman Catholic diocese and has a large Muslim population and a university. Its symbol is an African baobab near the coast, 21 metres round, said to be 700 to 800 years old; it was once used for important meetings and, in the nineteenth century, for executions. The climate is tropical savanna, with a wet season from November to mid-April.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahajanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With it he took the east-coast port of Toamasina in 1817, campaigned against the coastal and southern peoples, and in 1824 captured <b>Mahajanga</b>, the chief port of the Sakalava kingdom of Boina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom took over the trading towns of the Antalaotra, Swahili-speaking Muslim merchants, and later centred on <b>Majunga</b> (Mahajanga).</blockquote><a href="/state/boina">Boina</a></li></ul></section>
